  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Prioritizing conservation landscapes for the Asian elephant in Assam, Northeast India</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will work to ensure that the northeastern state of Assam, India retains threat-free interconnected populations of wild Asian elephants. This project will work in collaboration with the Assam Forest Department to obtain a comprehensive status assessment of the elephants in multiple conservation landscapes in Assam and use this information to collaboratively draft an implementable action plan for the species. Project objectives include: 1) assess elephant distribution and habitat across Assam; 2) map threat distribution; and 3) prioritize conservation action and areas for wild elephants. Specific project activities include: 1) develop a reliable and feasible protocol for a comprehensive status assessment of elephants; 2) map extant habitat and critical corridors for the species across Assam; 3) conduct an elephant distribution and threats survey; 4) identify areas of humanelephant conflict and non-natural mortality; 5) conduct stakeholder workshops for capacity building, dissemination of knowledge and formulation of action plans; and 6) draft a state-wide landscape-scale conservation action plan for elephants in collaboration with the State Government and other stakeholders. This will be the first statewide conservation status assessment for elephants in Assam, and lead to the development of a protocol for such assessments that is replicable across the species range.</narrative>
  </description>
